From: djsaunde <1245942+djsaunde@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 11:59:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 12/16] fast: clip triangles against the near and side planes
 as F3DEX does

LUS draws with GL_DEPTH_CLAMP and no near-plane clipping, the behaviour of
the NoN ("no near clip") microcodes. F3DEX and F3DLP clip: a triangle that
crosses the near plane (z = -w) or a side plane at the clip ratio
(x, y = +-ratio * w, G_MW_CLIP / gSPClipRatio) is cut there by the RSP,
and only the part in front reaches the RDP. Fast3D drew the whole
polygon, so a camera passing through a model showed big slabs of it
where the N64 shows thin slivers (1080's title demo: the rider's body at
steps 258, 2677, 3140, 4870).

With gfx_set_rsp_clipping(true), GfxSpTri1 clips such a triangle in clip
space (Sutherland-Hodgman over the five planes; no far plane, as on the
RSP: depth clamp still handles z > w) and draws the polygon as a fan.
A new vertex takes position, texture coordinates and colour linearly in
clip space, as the RSP's clipper does; its fog factor is computed again
from its own z / w. The clip ratio is read from G_MW_CLIP (RNX) in the
F3D moveword handler; default 2. Rectangles are not clipped. Off by
default: nothing changes until a port turns it on.

The polygon is drawn as a fan around its last vertex. Compared with the
N64's RDP command stream (n64ref, 1080's title demo step 4382) the RSP's
fan of a clipped ground triangle pivots there; with screen-space linear
shading (next patch) the pivot decides the colours, and the first vertex
as pivot was up to 16 levels off on near-camera slopes.

This matters beyond the near plane once shading is screen-space linear
(the next patch): the attributes at the clip edges are the RSP's, not
the GPU's.
